  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how TGFβ drives changes in both stromal fibroblasts and endothelial cells in the tumor microenvironment. In the upper portion, a resting fibroblast is shown becoming activated and then differentiating toward a myofibroblast state, ultimately contributing to a cancer-associated fibroblast phenotype. This transition is linked to increased extracellular matrix production, migration, and release of pro-tumorigenic, pro-metastatic, and pro-angiogenic factors. The diagram also highlights broader immune-related effects, including immunosuppression and immune exclusion, along with increased TGFβ expression and activation.\n\nIn parallel, the lower portion shows endothelial cells responding to TGFβ by promoting angiogenesis. A simple endothelial layer develops a sprouting structure, representing new vessel formation and increased migratory behavior. The associated text indicates enhanced angiogenesis, migration, increased TGFβ signaling, and PDGF-mediated recruitment of pericytes.\n\nOverall, the figure conveys that TGFβ acts as a central regulator connecting fibroblast activation and vascular remodeling. These coordinated effects appear to reinforce tumor progression by remodeling tissue structure, promoting blood vessel growth, and creating a microenvironment that supports cancer spread while limiting effective immune responses.",
